    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d276">

                <pb facs="0384" n="372" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg372"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.24/3–1246: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Iran</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Murray</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-03-22T20:00:00-05:00">March 22, 1946—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>236. Urtel 318, Mar. 12.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d276-f1"/> Present policy
                    precludes sale of surplus arms, ammunition and instruments of war to
                        Iran.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d276-f2"/> While Dept is prepared to consider
                    amendment of policy to permit sales to Iran upon receipt from Iranian Govt of
                    firm request specifying exact materials <pb facs="0385" n="373" xml:id="pg373"/>
                    desired, no assurance can be given azt this time that approval would be granted.
                    Desired material might, of course, be available from private manufacturers.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[In telegrams 531 and 538, both dated March 23, 1946, the Department
                        requested Moscow to send in full text “the most literal and careful
                        translation” of the Soviet note of November 29, 1945, to the United States
                        concerning the withdrawal of foreign troops from Iran. The request was made
                        “for purposes of verification, particularly part containing implication that
                        1942 treaty is only controlling factor.” (861.24591/3—2346) For text of the
                        Soviet note, see telegram 4015, November 30, 1945, from Moscow, <ref
                            target="frus:frus1945v08/pg_468">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, volume VIII, page
                            468</ref>. The nature of the reply by Moscow is given in footnote 84,
                            <ref target="frus:frus1945v08/pg_469">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, page 469</ref>.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d276-f1" n="38" xml:id="d276-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it stated that Maj. Gen. Clarence S. Ridley, Chief of the United
                    States Military Mission with the Iranian Army, had made formal inquiry of the
                    Embassy concerning sale by the United States to Iran of military equipment
                    essential for the maintenance of security involving its armed tribes
                    (891.24/3–1246).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d276-f2" n="39" xml:id="d276-f2-content">
                <p>For the Department’s “present policy” on disposal of military-type surplus
                    equipment to foreign governments, see Secretary’s Staff Committee document
                    SC/R–184, February 5, 1946, <ref target="frus1946v01">volume <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>
                    </ref>, documentation on United States National Security Policy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
